+# ECE141 to RedByte Basys3 Compatibility Matrix
+
+Status: Draft v1 (2026-02-18)  
+Owner: Connor Angiel
+
+## Scope
+
+This document maps the provided ECE141 Lab 1-8 handouts and three manuals to RedByte IDE product requirements.
+
+Product loop target:
+1. Design in RedByte IDE
+2. Verify deterministically in RedByte
+3. Export Vivado-ready artifacts
+4. Run in Vivado
+5. Program Basys3 hardware
+
+## Source Inputs
+
+Course lab PDFs:
+- `fac_jung002_ECE141_Lab1 (1).pdf`
+- `fac_jung002_ECE141_Lab2.pdf`
+- `fac_jung002_ECE141_Lab3.pdf`
+- `fac_jung002_ECE141_Lab4.pdf`
+- `fac_jung002_ECE141_Lab5.pdf`
+- `fac_jung002_ECE141_Lab6.pdf`
+- `fac_jung002_ECE141_Lab7.pdf`
+- `fac_jung002_ECE141_Lab8.pdf`
+
+Reference manuals:
+- `ECE348_GECE598_Refer_VHDL_quick_start.pdf`
+- `vivado-getting-started-en-us-2025.1.pdf`
+- `basys3_rm (1).pdf`
+
+Internal RedByte export/manifest contract references:
+- `packages/rb-apps/src/fpga/boards/basys3/basys3Bundle.ts`
+- `packages/rb-apps/src/fpga/boards/basys3/basys3ExportService.ts`
+- `packages/rb-apps/src/export/fileTreeManifest.ts`
+
+## Translation Rule (Legacy Lab Stack to RedByte Stack)
+
+The lab PDFs are written around Xilinx ISE + Spartan-3 era flow. RedByte target flow is Vivado + Basys3.  
+For compatibility, RedByte must preserve lab intent while translating tools and board mapping:
+
+- ISE schematic capture -> RedByte Design mode (graph/canvas)
+- HDL Bencher waveform checks -> RedByte Verify mode (deterministic vectors + diff + hash)
+- iMPACT/ISE programming -> Vivado/openFPGALoader Basys3 programming flow
+- Spartan-3 board pin assignment tables -> Basys3 pin map contract
+
+## Per-Lab Compatibility Matrix
+
+| Lab | Course expectation from handout | What RedByte should replace/simplify | What RedByte must still output/prove | Current parity note |
+| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
+| Lab 1 (Basic gates) | Draw simple gates, simulate, build truth table, verify outputs, basic hardware familiarity. | Use Design mode gate palette and Verify vectors instead of ISE + HDL Bencher setup steps. | Deterministic verify result, failure diff, and exportable `top.vhd` + `top.xdc` + `README.txt`. | High parity path. This is directly aligned with current RedByte core loop. |
+| Lab 2 (4-bit adder + hierarchy) | Build full-adder macro and hierarchical 4-bit adder; verify cases. | Use reusable subcircuits/components and deterministic verify vectors instead of ISE macro wizard flow. | Correct multi-bit behavior in Verify and Vivado-ready export with stable port naming and deterministic artifact ordering. | Mostly aligned; requires strong submodule UX and bus clarity in Design mode. |
+| Lab 3 (7-seg driver) | Build SSD driver, complete truth table/equations, assign pins, synthesize, implement, generate bitstream, program board. | Keep same logic objective but replace ISE process with RedByte Export readiness panel + Vivado handoff. | Valid SSD-related pin mapping, export that Vivado can place/route, and hardware-visible SSD behavior. | Partial today. Existing Basys3 bundle path is centered on `SW*` and `LD*`; seven-segment parity must be closed. |
+| Lab 4 (Simplified ALU) | Implement opcode-selected ALU (AND/NAND/OR/NOR/XOR/XNOR/Sum/Carry) with enable semantics; synthesize and hardware test. | Use Design mode + Verify mode opcode-driven vectors instead of manual ISE-only checks. | Vector proof for every opcode row and export-valid pin mapping for selected I/O. | High for logic verification, medium for board mapping UX polish. |
+| Lab 5 (2s complement add/sub + SSD) | Top-level arithmetic unit plus SSD display path; include Overflow/C_out/Sign and board integration. | Use structured Project and Export validation to catch missing arithmetic outputs and unmapped ports early. | Deterministic arithmetic verification plus export with full mapped outputs and clear README pin table. | Partial. Arithmetic path is feasible, but SSD-focused board mapping parity is still a gap. |
+| Lab 6 (Latches/flip-flops) | Verify D latch, D FF, T FF, JK FF behavior with simulation evidence. | Verify mode should provide deterministic sequential traces and clear timing-step semantics. | Clock/reset semantics in verify traces, deterministic PASS/FAIL, and optional testbench parity for Vivado sim. | Partial. Core sequential simulation exists; testbench generation and sequential UX should be hardened. |
+| Lab 7 (Synchronous counters) | Build 4-bit counter with Load/CE/reset/CEO, then BCD counter variant; provide state table/equations/simulation/hardware test. | Verify mode should provide counter-state progression checks and blocked-state guidance if clock mapping is missing. | Deterministic sequential vector run proving counter sequence and export-ready clock/reset mapping. | Partial. Counter logic is supported conceptually; robust clock/contract enforcement must remain strict. |
+| Lab 8 (Security lock FSM) | Build Moore FSM + counters, evaluate 12-bit stream in 3-bit windows, detect required sequences (`010`/`100`), show open indicator and count. | Use Design + Verify + Project readiness workflow instead of spread-out manual checks. | Deterministic sequential verification artifacts proving accepted/rejected streams and complete export mapping for hardware indicators. | Partial to medium. Requires strong FSM verification UX and full I/O mapping confidence for final hardware demo parity. |
+
+## Manual-to-Product Contract Matrix
+
+| Manual | Practical requirement | RedByte contract impact |
+| --- | --- | --- |
+| `ECE348_GECE598_Refer_VHDL_quick_start.pdf` | Reinforces VHDL entity/architecture/process semantics and simulation/synthesis split. | Export and import paths must preserve synthesizable structure and deterministic simulation meaning. |
+| `vivado-getting-started-en-us-2025.1.pdf` | Vivado is authoritative ISE replacement; IDE + Tcl/batch flow are first-class. | Export mode should remain compiler-like and produce consistent Vivado handoff artifacts and logs. |
+| `basys3_rm (1).pdf` | Board facts: XC7A35T-1CPG236C, 16 switches, 16 LEDs, 5 pushbuttons, 4-digit seven-segment, 100 MHz clock, JTAG programming path. | Basys3 board profile and pin map gates must stay canonical and complete for course parity. |
+
+## Canonical RedByte Artifact Contract for ECE141
+
+For each lab submission flow, RedByte should be able to produce:
+- `top.vhd`
+- `top.xdc`
+- `README.txt`
+- `testbench.vhd` when vectors exist
+- Determinism hash for verify/export run identity
+
+These names are already represented in RedByte export and file-manifest code paths.
+
+## High-Risk Gaps to Close for Full Semester Parity
+
+1. Expand export pin alias coverage beyond switch/LED-only paths where needed for seven-segment and button-heavy labs.
+2. Replace placeholder testbench output with real deterministic testbench generation for sequential labs.
+3. Keep strict blocked-state messaging when clock or critical mappings are missing (no silent success).
+4. Add lab-oriented verify presets/fixtures for Labs 3-8 so failure diffs map to expected classroom deliverables.
+
+## Acceptance Criteria for Matrix Usage
+
+This matrix is considered actionable when:
+1. Every Phase 1/2 IDE feature ticket references at least one row in this matrix.
+2. Export and verify gates include at least one check tied to each lab category (combinational, arithmetic, sequential, FSM/display).
+3. RedByte docs for student flow remain consistent with this matrix and Basys3-only scope.
